\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The signal-to-noise problem in EEG}

Scalp EEG recovers signals of the order of 1--50~\textmu{}V
from an envelope of 10--100~\textmu{}V of background
activity and movement artifact. The dominant strategy for isolating a
cognitive signal is time-domain averaging: present a stimulus many
times, epoch the EEG, and average the epochs. Signal-to-noise ratio
(SNR) then grows as $\sqrt{N}$ where $N$ is the trial count, producing
the familiar event-related potentials --- N170, P300, N400 --- each of
which typically requires 100 or more trials and 15 to 25 minutes of
recording per condition.

\subsection{The frequency-tagging alternative}

An alternative, developed over decades in vision science, exploits the
fact that a sinusoidally or periodically modulated stimulus evokes a
response at the same frequency. If stimulation is at $f$~Hz, the
cortical response concentrates at $f$~Hz and its harmonics; noise in
the EEG is broadband ($\sim 1/f$ plus measurement). A narrow-band SNR
estimate therefore yields a dramatic signal-to-noise gain without
trial averaging. \citet{norcia2015jvis} review the full SSVEP literature
and codify the analysis principles.

The insight that matters here is that \emph{two} frequencies can be
tagged simultaneously. If standard stimuli appear at a base frequency
$F$ and oddball stimuli appear at $F/n$, then a cortical response at
$F/n$ and its harmonics specifically indicates discrimination between
standard and oddball --- without any behavioural task. This is the
core of both the Rossion face-individuation paradigm and the Stothart
Fastball memory assessment.

<<fig-timing-diagram, echo=False, fig=True, width='\linewidth', caption='Schematic timing of a frequency-tagged oddball paradigm. Every 5th standard is replaced by an oddball, yielding responses at the base rate $F$ and at the oddball rate $F/5$. Top bar: stimulus stream. Bottom traces: idealised amplitude spectra as base rate rises from 3 Hz (Stothart Fastball) to 5.88 Hz (Rossion FPVS).'>>=
fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(
    2, 1, figsize=(8, 4.0),
    gridspec_kw={"height_ratios": [1, 2]},
    constrained_layout=True,
)

# Top: cartoon stimulus timeline.
ax1.set_xlim(0, 10)
ax1.set_ylim(-0.2, 1.2)
ax1.set_yticks([])
for i in range(10):
    colour = "tab:red" if (i + 1) % 5 == 0 else "grey"
    ax1.fill_between([i, i + 0.5], 0, 1, color=colour, alpha=0.8)
ax1.set_xlabel("Stimulus index")
ax1.set_title("Standards (grey) with an oddball every 5th item (red)")

# Bottom: idealised spectra for the two paradigms.
f = np.linspace(0, 10, 2001)
def spectrum(base, oddball, amp_base=1.0, amp_odd=0.4, sigma=0.04):
    s = np.zeros_like(f)
    for k in range(1, 4):
        s += amp_base * np.exp(-((f - k*base)**2) / (2*sigma**2))
    for k in range(1, 5):
        s += amp_odd * np.exp(-((f - k*oddball)**2) / (2*sigma**2))
    return s + 0.05 * np.random.default_rng(0).standard_normal(f.size)

ax2.plot(f, spectrum(3.0, 0.6), label="Stothart: base 3 Hz, oddball 0.6 Hz", color="tab:blue")
ax2.plot(f, spectrum(5.88, 1.176) + 0.8, label="Rossion: base 5.88 Hz, oddball 1.18 Hz", color="tab:green")
ax2.set_xlim(0, 10)
ax2.set_xlabel("Frequency (Hz)")
ax2.set_ylabel("|FFT| (a.u.)")
ax2.legend(loc="upper right")
ax2.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
@

\section{Steady-state visual evoked potentials (SSVEP)}
\label{sec:ssvep}

\subsection{Definition and taxonomy}

The SSVEP is the cortical response to periodic visual stimulation,
stationary in the frequency domain. \citet{norcia2015jvis} distinguish:

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item \textbf{On--off flicker} at a fixed frequency (most common in BCI and
    attention work).
    \item \textbf{Continuous sinusoidal contrast modulation}, preferred in
    Rossion's face-individuation paradigm for its gentler spectral leakage.
    \item \textbf{Multi-frequency tagging}, in which different stimulus elements
    flicker at different rates to permit simultaneous tracking of attentional
    state across competing streams.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Why SSVEP beats trial-averaged ERP on SNR}

Let $x(t)$ be EEG recorded during steady-state stimulation with period $T = 1/f$.
The signal is present at $f$ and its harmonics $\{2f, 3f, \ldots\}$ as a line
spectrum; the background EEG is approximately $1/f$-distributed with broadband
noise overlaid. A narrow-band SNR estimate

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SNR}(f) = \frac{|X(f)|}{\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{f'\in B} |X(f')|}
\end{equation}

\noindent with $B$ a set of neighbouring non-target bins, grows with recording
duration approximately as $\sqrt{T}$ (because the DFT bin width narrows as
$1/T$), and the line component grows linearly in amplitude. This is why
60--180~s of SSVEP data can match or exceed the per-subject SNR of a 20~min
averaged-ERP study.

\subsection{Typical parameters}

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item Base frequencies in the literature span 2--40~Hz;
    face-specific work uses 5--6~Hz (see
    Section~\ref{sec:fpvs}).
    \item Session durations of 60--180~s per condition are
    standard.
    \item Electrodes: medial occipital (Oz) for generic SSVEP; lateral
    occipito-temporal (PO7/PO8/P9/P10) for face-specific responses.
\end{itemize}

\section{FPVS face individuation (Rossion lab)}
\label{sec:fpvs}

\subsection{Paradigm}

\citet{liushuang2014} introduced an oddball variant of FPVS for unfamiliar
face individuation. An image of a single unfamiliar base identity is
presented at 5.88~Hz (170~ms SOA); every fifth stimulus is swapped
for a different unfamiliar identity, yielding an oddball rate of
1.176~Hz. A response at the oddball frequency and its harmonics
indicates that the visual system discriminates the newly-presented identity
from the repeated base.

\subsection{Key empirical results}

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item A robust oddball response is recorded over \emph{right}
    occipito-temporal cortex (PO8, P10), convergent with the known
    right-hemisphere dominance of face identity processing.
    \item Intracerebral recordings localise the response to inferior
    occipital and fusiform gyri.
    \item The paradigm has now been applied in more than twenty published
    studies covering prosopagnosia, autism spectrum conditions, infant face
    processing, and cross-cultural face recognition.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stimuli}

The canonical Liu-Shuang stimuli are 50 unfamiliar face identities,
neutral expression, cropped to exclude external features, presented
against a grey background at 11\,\textdegree\ $\times$ 9\,\textdegree\ of
visual angle. These stimuli are not publicly distributed due to
subject-image rights. Open alternatives with comparable norming
include the Chicago Face Database and KDEF; custom sets compiled from
either can reproduce the paradigm faithfully with minor re-norming.

<<fig-rossion-spectrum, echo=False, fig=True, width='0.9\linewidth', caption='Idealised Rossion FPVS oddball response at right occipito-temporal cortex. Red dashed line: base 5.88 Hz. Blue dashed lines: oddball 1.176 Hz and harmonics. The oddball response is the unambiguous neural signature of face individuation.'>>=
f = np.linspace(0, 10, 2001)
spec = np.zeros_like(f)
for k in range(1, 3):
    spec += 1.0 * np.exp(-((f - k*5.88)**2) / (2*0.04**2))
for k in range(1, 5):
    spec += 0.45 * np.exp(-((f - k*1.176)**2) / (2*0.04**2))
spec += 0.05 * np.random.default_rng(1).standard_normal(f.size)

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 3.2), constrained_layout=True)
ax.plot(f, spec, color="black", linewidth=0.9)
ax.axvline(5.88, color="red", linestyle="--", linewidth=1, label="base 5.88 Hz")
for k in range(1, 5):
    ax.axvline(1.176*k, color="blue", linestyle=":", linewidth=0.8, alpha=0.7)
ax.set_xlabel("Frequency (Hz)")
ax.set_ylabel("|FFT| (a.u.)")
ax.set_title("Rossion FPVS face individuation (schematic spectrum, right OT channel)")
ax.legend(loc="upper right")
ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
@

\section{Fastball memory assessment (Stothart et al.)}
\label{sec:fastball}

\subsection{Paradigm}

The Fastball line of work has a clear pre-history:
\citet{stothart2017neuropsych} first established that SSVEP
frequency-tagging can index \emph{semantic} category discrimination
implicitly (for example, living vs.\ non-living, familiar vs.\
unfamiliar). \citet{milton2020scirep} then showed that the paradigm
indexes preserved semantic memory in healthy ageing, confirming that
the readout is genuinely memory-sensitive rather than purely sensory.

\citet{stothart2020neuroimage} formalised this as the
\emph{Fastball} paradigm: a recognition-memory variant of frequency-tagged
oddball. Every-day colour objects drawn from the Bank of Standardised
Stimuli v2.0~\citep{brodeur2010boss} are shown at a base rate of 3~Hz
(166~ms image + 166~ms ISI). In the recognition condition, every fifth
image is an item that the participant has been pre-exposed to moments
earlier; the oddball rate is 0.6~Hz. An above-baseline response at
0.6~Hz and harmonics demonstrates that the cortex differentiates seen
from unseen items, \emph{with no behavioural response required}.
\citet{nurdal2021ijp} dissect the role of repetition priming in the
same paradigm, establishing that the Fastball response is not reducible
to low-level image-repetition effects.

\subsection{Application to dementia}

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item \citet{stothart2021brain} applied Fastball to twenty Alzheimer's
    patients, twenty healthy older adults, and twenty younger adults. The
    paradigm distinguished AD from healthy older adults with
    \mbox{AUC $= 0.86$} ($p < 0.001$), whereas a behavioural two-alternative
    forced-choice task achieved only AUC $= 0.63$ ($p = 0.148$) on the same
    cohort.
    \item \citet{stothart2025braincomms} extended the paradigm to 107
    participants including amnestic and non-amnestic mild cognitive impairment.
    Fastball responses in aMCI were reduced relative to controls, supporting
    use as an early biomarker of amnestic dysfunction.
    \item Home-use trials are ongoing in collaboration with BRACE
    Alzheimer's Research; results are forthcoming in 2026.
    \item \citet{hermann2025adlbd} extended the assessment beyond
    amnestic impairment to Lewy Body Dementia, applying the frequency-
    tagging readout to \emph{visual} processing deficits characteristic
    of LBD rather than memory deficits characteristic of AD.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Beyond memory: paradigm extensions}

Recent work has shown that the Fastball machinery is not limited to
recognition memory. \citet{hermann2025neuropsych} used the same
frequency-tagged oddball design to quantify implicit discrimination of
\emph{line orientation}, a low-level visual feature with no explicit
memory component. The same SNR / harmonic-summation analysis applies
unchanged; only the stimuli differ. This generalisation matters for
the project at hand: a single paradigm skeleton can serve multiple
clinical and basic-science questions by swapping the standard/oddball
stimulus category, without re-engineering the timing, acquisition or
analysis.

\subsection{Why it works for memory specifically}

The Fastball oddball is a \emph{memory-modulated} stimulus: whether a given
image counts as ``standard'' or ``oddball'' is determined by the participant's
history, not by any low-level visual feature. Discriminability in the
frequency-domain response therefore cannot be inherited from basic sensory
mismatch --- it requires access to encoding and retrieval circuitry. In
Alzheimer's disease the retrieval disruption is profound enough that the
oddball response collapses even when patients still score above chance on
overt tests, which is precisely what makes the paradigm a useful early
biomarker.

<<fig-fastball-spectrum, echo=False, fig=True, width='0.9\linewidth', caption="Idealised Stothart Fastball spectrum at Oz/Pz. Red dashed line: base 3 Hz. Blue dashed lines: oddball 0.6 Hz and harmonics. In Alzheimer's disease (dashed trace) the oddball response amplitude is markedly reduced relative to healthy older adults (solid trace), while the base-rate response is preserved.">>=
f = np.linspace(0, 6, 2001)
def fastball_spec(strength, seed):
    s = np.zeros_like(f)
    for k in range(1, 3):
        s += 1.0 * np.exp(-((f - k*3.0)**2) / (2*0.04**2))
    for k in range(1, 6):
        s += strength * np.exp(-((f - k*0.6)**2) / (2*0.04**2))
    s += 0.05 * np.random.default_rng(seed).standard_normal(f.size)
    return s

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 3.2), constrained_layout=True)
ax.plot(f, fastball_spec(0.55, 2), color="black", linewidth=1.0, label="Healthy older adult")
ax.plot(f, fastball_spec(0.12, 3), color="tab:red", linewidth=1.0, linestyle="--", label="Alzheimer's disease")
ax.axvline(3.0, color="red", linestyle="--", linewidth=1, alpha=0.5)
for k in range(1, 6):
    ax.axvline(0.6*k, color="blue", linestyle=":", linewidth=0.7, alpha=0.5)
ax.set_xlabel("Frequency (Hz)")
ax.set_ylabel("|FFT| (a.u.)")
ax.set_title("Fastball recognition-memory oddball (schematic, Oz/Pz)")
ax.legend(loc="upper right")
ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
@

\section{Shared methodology: analysis of a frequency-tagged oddball}
\label{sec:methods}

Across all three paradigms, the analysis pipeline is largely shared.

\subsection{Pre-processing}

\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item Notch filter at line frequency (50~Hz or 60~Hz).
    \item Bandpass 0.1 to 100~Hz (broad enough to retain harmonics).
    \item Artifact rejection by amplitude threshold or component-based methods
    (ICA for multi-channel montages; simple threshold for 8-channel dry-electrode
    devices such as the g.tec Unicorn).
    \item Crop to integer multiples of the oddball period so the FFT has bins
    exactly at the target frequencies (avoids spectral leakage).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Spectral analysis}

Compute the FFT of the continuous pre-processed record per channel. Extract
amplitudes at the target (base and oddball) frequencies and their harmonics.

\subsection{Signal-to-noise estimation}

The Liu-Shuang / Stothart convention is

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SNR}(f_t) \;=\; \frac{|X(f_t)|}{\frac{1}{|N|}\sum_{f \in N} |X(f)|}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $N$ is the set of neighbouring bins within $\pm 0.10$~Hz of
the target, excluding the immediately-adjacent bins. A z-score of the target
bin against the noise distribution, $z > 1.96$, corresponds to $p < 0.05$
uncorrected and is the standard per-harmonic significance threshold.

\subsection{Harmonic summation}

Summing SNR across harmonics $\{f_t, 2f_t, 3f_t, \ldots\}$ increases
sensitivity when the response contains energy beyond the fundamental ---
which is the norm in visual oddball paradigms. Stothart sums ten harmonics
up to 7.2~Hz; Rossion sums four to five harmonics up to
5~Hz.

\section{Practical implications for low-channel EEG}

The g.tec Unicorn Hybrid Black provides eight fixed dry electrodes
(Fz, C3, Cz, C4, Pz, PO7, Oz, PO8) at 250~Hz. This is
materially sparser than the 65--128 channel research montages in the
primary papers, but it is sufficient for:

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item \textbf{SSVEP generic}: Oz and the parieto-occipital ring
    (PO7/PO8) are the primary SSVEP sites --- the Unicorn covers all
    three.
    \item \textbf{Rossion face FPVS}: PO8 proxies the right
    occipito-temporal peak reported by Liu-Shuang. PO7 provides the
    left-hemisphere control. Oz captures the base-rate response.
    Missing: P10 / TP10, which sit ventrally relative to PO8 and
    capture additional face-response variance but are not essential.
    \item \textbf{Stothart Fastball}: Oz and Pz cover the mid-occipital/
    parietal ridge where Stothart reports peak oddball amplitude.
\end{itemize}

In this project, the new paradigm modules
(\texttt{eegnb.experiments.fpvs\_stothart.VisualFPVSStothart} and
\texttt{eegnb.experiments.fpvs\_rossion.VisualFPVSRossion}) expose the
paradigm-appropriate region of interest as a
\texttt{channels\_of\_interest} attribute, which downstream analysis
code resolves to brainflow channel indices via
\texttt{eegnb.utils.rois.resolve\_roi}. Because the Unicorn's hardware
montage is fixed, this amounts to a software-side ROI selection rather
than a physical reconfiguration.

\section{Limitations and open questions}

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
    \item \textbf{Display timing.} Frequency-tagged paradigms require
    precise per-frame timing; lab-grade CRTs or gaming-grade LCDs at
    refresh rates that are integer multiples of the target frequency
    are preferred. On Wayland-native compositors (Fedora 43 etc.)
    XWayland introduces frame jitter of the order of the monitor's
    vblank interval; whether this is tolerable depends on the target
    precision.
    \item \textbf{Dry electrodes.} Stothart's validation was run on a
    65-channel HydroCel Geodesic net with impedances
    <50~k$\Omega$. Dry-electrode devices such as the Unicorn
    start an order of magnitude higher and are more sensitive to
    motion artifact. Expect to need more trials/longer sessions than
    the published minimums.
    \item \textbf{Reference identities.} Rossion's paradigm as
    published fixes the base identity throughout a sequence. Replacing
    the base with a pool of identities changes the inference: the
    oddball response then indexes discrimination between two pools,
    not between a specific identity and the rest.
    \item \textbf{Clinical translation.} Stothart's Alzheimer's result
    is impressive at AUC $= 0.86$ but is based on a relatively small
    sample; community-wide validation and home-use data are still
    emerging.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Three paradigms, one trick: periodic stimulation turns a trial-averaging
problem into a narrow-band SNR problem. SSVEP demonstrates the
principle, Rossion FPVS adapts it to category-level discrimination
with a face-identity oddball, and Stothart's Fastball extends it into
a clinically viable memory assay. For small-channel dry-electrode EEG
systems such as the g.tec Unicorn, all three remain tractable with
minor ROI accommodations. The shared logic means the underlying
analysis pipeline --- FFT, SNR at target plus harmonics, z-score against
neighbouring bins --- is portable across paradigms with minimal code
change.

